---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import {FAQSection, RelatedArticles} from '../../components/article';

const title = 'Best Accounting Software for Freelancers in 2025: Complete Guide';
const description = 'Compare the top accounting software for freelancers. We tested QuickBooks, FreshBooks, Wave, and more to find the best bookkeeping tools for self-employed professionals.';
const author = 'FloatJet Team';
const authorUrl = '/about';
const pubDate = new Date('2024-12-25');
const readingTime = 18;
const image = '/images/unsplash/photo-1554224155-6726b3ff858f-1200w.webp';
const imageAlt = 'Calculator and financial documents representing accounting software';

const headings = [
  {depth: 2, slug: 'why-you-need-this', text: 'Why You Need This'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'what-to-look-for', text: 'What to Look For'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'top-picks', text: 'The Eight Tools Worth Considering'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'tax-stuff', text: 'The Tax Stuff'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'mobile-apps', text: 'Mobile Apps'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'who-should-use-what', text: 'Who Should Use What'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'faq', text: 'Questions'},
  {depth: 2, slug: 'my-picks', text: 'My Picks'}
];

const faqData = [
  {
    question: 'Do I actually need accounting software?',
    answer: 'Even with simple finances, yes. Saves hours in April. Catches deductions you\'d miss. Professional invoices get you paid faster. And you finally know if you\'re actually making money. The software usually costs less than the time and money it saves.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Accounting software vs invoicing software?',
    answer: 'Invoicing just sends invoices. Accounting software does that plus expense tracking, reports, bank connections, tax prep. Most freelancers need the full thing. Invoicing alone doesn\'t tell you if you\'re profitable.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Free options work?',
    answer: 'Wave is genuinely good for free. Straightforward needs, basic bookkeeping, it handles it. Trade-off is no time tracking, limited integrations, and email-only support. But for zero dollars? Hard to argue.'
  },
  {
    question: 'QuickBooks vs FreshBooks?',
    answer: 'QuickBooks is better at tax prep and expense categorization. FreshBooks is better at invoicing, time tracking, and client management. Tax optimization priority? Go QuickBooks. Getting paid and managing projects? FreshBooks.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Accountant or software?',
    answer: 'Both. Software handles daily bookkeeping. An accountant provides strategy and catches things you\'d miss. Use software all year, talk to an accountant quarterly or at tax time. The software makes their job easier and cheaper.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What do accountants prefer?',
    answer: 'Most know QuickBooks or Xero. Ask yours before choosing. But any decent software exports what they need. Convenience matters more than which logo is on the app.'
  }
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Best Invoicing Tools for Remote Professionals',
    excerpt: 'Get paid faster with the right invoicing software.',
    href: '/tools/best-invoicing-tools',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1450101499163-c8848c66ca85-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Invoice and payment documents'
  },
  {
    title: 'Tax Tips for Remote Workers',
    excerpt: 'Navigate taxes as a remote worker or digital nomad.',
    href: '/blog/tax-tips-remote-workers',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1586486855514-8c633cc6fd38-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Tax forms and calculator'
  },
  {
    title: 'Best Banking for Digital Nomads',
    excerpt: 'Find the right bank account for your freelance business.',
    href: '/blog/best-banking-digital-nomads',
    image: '/images/unsplash/photo-1601597111158-2fceff292cdc-800w.webp',
    imageAlt: 'Digital banking on mobile device'
  }
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="tools/finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    My first year freelancing I did my taxes with a spreadsheet and a shoebox of receipts. Took me two full weekends.
    Missed at least two grand in deductions I found out about later. Never again.
  </p>

  <p>
    Good accounting software fixes that. Turns the April nightmare into something almost painless. Saves you money on
    deductions you'd otherwise forget. And honestly? Helps you actually understand if your freelance thing is working.
  </p>

  <p>I've tested eight options over the past year. Here's what actually matters.</p>

  <h2 id="why-you-need-this">Why You Need This</h2>

  <p>
    Look, when you're self-employed, you're also the entire finance department. One person. That's a lot of balls to
    juggle.
  </p>

  <p>
    Without a system you'll miss write-offs. That coffee meeting with a potential client. The software subscription you
    forgot about. Your home office setup. All deductible. All easy to lose track of.
  </p>

  <p>
    Quarterly taxes? Most freelancers underestimate them. Then April shows up with penalties. Surprise.
  </p>

  <p>
    Who owes you money right now? Are they late? Do you actually know? Most freelancers don't. Not really.
  </p>

  <p>
    And visibility into whether you're actually profitable vs just busy? That requires actual numbers. Not vibes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-to-look-for">What to Look For</h2>

  <p>
    The basics that matter. Can it send invoices? Track expenses and categorize them? Connect to your bank so you're not
    doing manual entry like some kind of masochist? Generate tax reports when you need them? Capture receipts on your
    phone?
  </p>

  <p>If a tool can't do those five things well, skip it.</p>

  <p>
    Nice extras include time tracking for hourly billing, project tracking so you know which clients are actually
    profitable, multi-currency for international work, mileage logging, and integrations with other stuff you use.
  </p>

  <p>
    Red flags? No bank connection means manual entry. Gross. No mobile app means you'll forget to track things. An
    interface clearly designed for accountants means you'll give up after a week. Hidden fees for basic features. And no
    export options that lock your data in forever.
  </p>

  <h2 id="top-picks">The Eight Tools Worth Considering</h2>

  <h3>QuickBooks Self-Employed</h3>
  <p>
    This is what I point most freelancers toward. Built specifically for us, not retrofitted from small business
    software.
  </p>

  <p>
    The tax stuff is where it shines. Swipe left or right on transactions to categorize them. Personal or business.
    Takes like thirty seconds a day. By April you've got everything sorted and your Schedule C practically writes
    itself.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>What I like.</strong> AI categorizes most stuff automatically. GPS tracks your mileage without you doing
    anything. Calculates quarterly tax estimates so you're not guessing. Exports straight to TurboTax if you use that.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>What bugs me.</strong> The invoicing is basic. If you send a lot of invoices or need project tracking,
    you'll want something else.
  </p>

  <p>Runs fifteen bucks monthly. Twenty-five if you want the TurboTax bundle which is honestly worth it.</p>

  <h3>FreshBooks</h3>
  <p>
    Started as invoicing software and honestly still the best at that part. Beautiful invoices. Clients actually
    compliment them sometimes. Weird flex but it happens.
  </p>

  <p>
    Payment reminders work automatically. Set them once and forget about it. No more awkward follow-up emails asking
    where your money is.
  </p>

  <p>
    Time tracking built in. Project profitability tracking so you know which clients are worth it. Client portal where
    they can view estimates and pay you. The app is excellent. Full features on mobile.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The downside?</strong> Client limits on cheaper plans. Seventeen bucks gets you five clients. Thirty gets
    you fifty. Fifty-five for unlimited. Adds up if you work with lots of people.
  </p>

  <h3>Wave</h3>
  <p>
    Completely free. Not free trial. Not freemium with annoying limits. Actually free. Forever.
  </p>

  <p>
    They make money on payment processing instead. Two point nine percent plus sixty cents per transaction. Which is
    pretty standard.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlimited invoicing. Bank connections. Financial reports. Receipt scanning. Everything you need for basic
    bookkeeping.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>The catch?</strong> No time tracking. Integrations are limited. Support is email only. But for free? Can't
    really complain.
  </p>

  <p>If you're just starting out or have simple finances, this is probably your first stop.</p>

  <h3>Xero</h3>
  <p>
    More powerful than most freelancers need. But if you're growing toward an agency or planning to hire people
    eventually, it scales.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlimited users on every plan. Over a thousand integrations. Bank reconciliation that actually learns your patterns.
    Multi-currency built in.
  </p>

  <p>
    Learning curve is steeper. The entry tier only lets you send twenty invoices monthly which is weird. And at
    forty-two to seventy-eight bucks monthly for the plans that matter, it's pricier than freelancer-focused options.
  </p>

  <p>Makes sense if you're thinking bigger picture. Overkill if you're a one-person show staying that way.</p>

  <h3>Bonsai</h3>
  <p>
    The everything tool. Contracts. Proposals. Invoicing. Accounting. Tax prep. All in one place.
  </p>

  <p>
    For freelancers who hate switching between apps, this is appealing. Send a proposal, turn it into a contract, send
    an invoice, track time, run reports. Same interface for all of it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Accounting features aren't as deep as QuickBooks or Xero. But if your finances are straightforward and you value
    having one tool instead of five, Bonsai works.
  </p>

  <p>Twenty-one bucks monthly to start. Thirty-two for the plan most freelancers want.</p>

  <h3>HoneyBook</h3>
  <p>
    Built for creative freelancers. Photographers. Designers. Anyone who needs beautiful proposals and client
    questionnaires.
  </p>

  <p>
    The client journey stuff is great. From first inquiry through final payment, everything flows together. Contracts
    look professional. Automation handles follow-ups.
  </p>

  <p>
    But it's not really accounting software. Expense tracking is minimal. No tax reports. You'll need to pair it with
    Wave or something else for actual bookkeeping.
  </p>

  <p>Sixteen bucks monthly gets you started.</p>

  <h3>Zoho Invoice</h3>
  <p>
    Solid and cheap. Part of the bigger Zoho ecosystem so it plays nice with their other products.
  </p>

  <p>Professional invoices. Time tracking. Payment reminders. Multi-currency. Everything works fine.</p>

  <p>
    Interface feels a bit dated compared to competitors. And it's invoicing only, so you'll need Zoho Books for full
    accounting.
  </p>

  <p>Free tier exists. Paid plans start at nine bucks monthly.</p>

  <h3>Fiverr Workspace</h3>
  <p>
    Used to be called AND.CO. Simpler than most competitors. Which is either exactly what you want or not enough.
  </p>

  <p>Contracts. Invoices. Time tracking. Expense tracking. Basic reports. Task management. All straightforward.</p>

  <p>Free if you're a Fiverr seller. Twenty-four bucks monthly otherwise.</p>

  <p>No tax reports. Better as an invoicing tool than a complete solution.</p>

  <h2 id="tax-stuff">The Tax Stuff</h2>

  <p>
    Quarterly estimates are the freelance tax trap. Most people don't know what to pay. Then they get hit with
    penalties.
  </p>

  <p>
    QuickBooks handles this automatically. Calculates your quarterly payment based on what you've earned and spent. Just
    do what it says.
  </p>

  <p>Bonsai does something similar with Schedule C previews.</p>

  <p>
    FreshBooks gives you reports to figure it out yourself. Everything else you're on your own or using separate tax
    software.
  </p>

  <p>
    For filing, QuickBooks exports directly to TurboTax. One click. FreshBooks and Wave export reports your accountant
    can work with. Xero has a bunch of tax software integrations.
  </p>

  <p>
    The deduction tracking matters most. Every receipt you capture is potential money back. QuickBooks nails this with
    the swipe interface. Makes it almost fun. Almost.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mobile-apps">Mobile Apps</h2>

  <p>
    If the app is bad you won't track expenses. Simple as that. You'll get a receipt, think "I'll add this later," and
    lose it forever.
  </p>

  <p>
    QuickBooks has the best mobile experience in my opinion. Receipt scanning works great. Mileage tracking runs in the
    background. Quick to add expenses.
  </p>

  <p>FreshBooks mobile is almost as good. Full invoicing. Expense tracking. Everything you need.</p>

  <p>HoneyBook's app focuses on client communication and invoicing.</p>

  <p>Wave and Xero work fine but aren't as polished. Bonsai has the core features.</p>

  <p>
    Look for receipt scanning that reads the info automatically, quick expense entry, invoice creation, mileage
    tracking, and payment notifications.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-should-use-what">Who Should Use What</h2>

  <h3>Writing or creating content?</h3>
  <p>
    FreshBooks or Bonsai. You need good invoicing, project tracking, and maybe contracts. Tax complexity is usually low.
  </p>

  <h3>Design or creative work?</h3>
  <p>
    HoneyBook or Bonsai. Client management matters. Questionnaires and beautiful proposals help win work. Pair HoneyBook
    with Wave for actual accounting.
  </p>

  <h3>Development or consulting?</h3>
  <p>
    FreshBooks or Xero. Higher income usually means more tax stuff to deal with. Solid expense tracking matters. Xero
    scales if you're growing toward an agency.
  </p>

  <h3>Gig work or side hustle?</h3>
  <p>
    QuickBooks Self-Employed or Wave. Separating personal and business expenses is the whole game. QuickBooks handles
    this elegantly. Wave works if you're disciplined about it.
  </p>

  <h3>International clients?</h3>
  <p>
    Xero or FreshBooks. Multi-currency support becomes essential when you're invoicing in different currencies. Xero has
    the edge for complex stuff.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2 id="my-picks">My Picks</h2>

  <p>
    Most freelancers should start with QuickBooks Self-Employed. Tax features are unmatched. Pays for itself in
    deductions caught.
  </p>

  <p>Want better invoicing? FreshBooks. Those automatic reminders alone are worth it.</p>

  <p>Starting out and broke? Wave. Free and genuinely functional.</p>

  <p>Want everything in one place? Bonsai. Contracts, invoicing, and accounting together.</p>

  <p>Planning to grow? Xero. Scales from solo to agency.</p>

  <p>Every one of these has a free trial. Take two for a spin before committing. The right tool saves you more than it
    costs.</p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
